 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gaussian Avatar Reconstruction](1575-gaussian-avatar-reconstruction.md)
-Gaussian Avatar Reconstruction is a family of methods that reconstruct animatable, dynamic 3D human or head avatars from video by representing them as collections of 3D Gaussians. It solves the problem of generating high-fidelity, real-time renderable avatars that can be driven by parametric body models (SMPL/FLAME) or pose features, serving as a dynamic alternative to static Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) or traditional mesh-based animation. It is distinguished from its sibling [Gaussian Splatting](946-gaussian-splatting.md) by its explicit dependency on a parametric structural prior to drive deformation, and from [Gaussian Avatars](1477-gaussian-avatars.md) which typically focus on head-only reconstruction using the FLAME model.
+Gaussian Avatar Reconstruction methods represent dynamic human subjects (typically heads or full bodies) as a set of deformable 3D Gaussian primitives driven by parametric models like FLAME or SMPL. Unlike static [Gaussian Splatting](946-gaussian-splatting.md) which assumes a rigid scene, this family solves the problem of rendering animatable, high-fidelity avatars from video by constraining Gaussian geometry to the parametric mesh surface and optimizing pose-dependent deformations. It differs from sibling methods like [Pose-free and Feed-forward Training](6500-pose-free-and-feed-forward-training.md) by explicitly relying on known or estimated camera poses and parametric priors for deformation, rather than learning implicit mappings from unposed images. Specific to this family: Gaussian positions and orientations are initialized and constrained to the surface of a parametric mesh (e.g., FLAME for heads, SMPL for bodies). Gaussian parameters are functions of the parametric model's pose and shape parameters. This allows the avatar to be animated by changing the pose/shape inputs. Gaussians are typically initialized per triangle or vertex of the parametric mesh.
 
 ### [Pose-free and Feed-forward Training](6500-pose-free-and-feed-forward-training.md)
-This family of methods eliminates the dependency on pre-computed camera poses (typically from Structure-from-Motion) by either predicting them jointly with the scene representation or by using a feed-forward network to generate Gaussian primitives directly from input images. It solves the problem of requiring calibrated input data, enabling reconstruction from uncalibrated, sparse, or casual image collections. Unlike standard [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3dgs-optimization.md) which assumes known poses, these methods treat camera parameters as either learnable variables or implicit outputs of a neural network.
+This family encompasses methods that reconstruct 3D Gaussian primitives from images without requiring pre-computed camera poses from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines like COLMAP. It solves the dependency on accurate initial pose estimation, enabling rapid reconstruction from casual, sparse, or uncalibrated image collections. It is distinguished from [Joint Pose Optimization](6888-joint-pose-optimization.md) by either eliminating pose variables entirely via feed-forward networks or fixing them during primary optimization, and from [Gaussian Avatar Reconstruction](1575-gaussian-avatar-reconstruction.md) by focusing on static scene geometry rather than articulated human models.
 
